            {
                "box": {
                    "id": "obj-33",
                    "linecount": 59,
                    "maxclass": "comment",
                    "numinlets": 1,
                    "numoutlets": 0,
                    "patching_rect": [ 30.0, 553.0, 580.0, 870.0 ],
                    "text": "================================================================================\nchorus_univibe_optical_throb — Parameter Information\n================================================================================\n\nOVERVIEW\nA standalone stereo model of the late-1960s Shin-ei Uni-Vibe Chorus/Vibrato foot pedal from Champ Darabundit, Russell Wedelich, and Pete Bischoff (DAFx-19 Paper 31, Sections 2, 3.1, 3.2 & 4.2, Eq. 1–21, Table 1, Fig. 2–8). Captures the three exact circuit discoveries from DAFx-19 that give the Uni-Vibe its iconic liquid \"double-beat\" throb rather than a generic phaser sweep:\n1. Staggered Phasing Capacitors Cp (Table 1: 0.015 uF, 0.22 uF, 470 pF, 0.0047 uF) so Stage 2 sweeps deep in the 4.5–185 Hz tuba fundamental region while Stages 1, 3, and 4 sweep the midrange and highs.\n2. Collector/Emitter Gain Imbalance (alpha_n != beta_n) + Blocking Capacitor C_DC (Eq. 4–21, Fig. 6): Turns each stage into a sweeping high-shelf + phase shifter H_cn(z) + H_en(z) whose low-frequency shelf throb interacts with the dry signal in Chorus mode.\n3. Asymmetric Darlington Emitter-Follower tanh Bias + DC Blocker (Fig. 7) and the fast-attack/slow-recovery incandescent lamp + CdS LDR resistance curve (Fig. 8).\n\nINLETS & OUTLETS\n- in1 / in2: Stereo Audio Input (Tuba; mono in1 auto-normalizes to stereo)\n- in3: Optional Envelope Modulator [0..1] (boosts incandescent bulb intensity on loud tuba attacks)\n- in4: Optional 1-Bar Audio-Rate Phasor [0..1] (locks the Uni-Vibe lamp cycle to host tempo when patched)\n- out1 / out2: Stereo Uni-Vibe Chorus/Vibrato Output\n\nPARAMETERS (6)\n\n1. pedal_speed_hz\n   - Range: 0.20 to 7.60 Hz (Uni-Vibe Foot-Pedal LFO Rate, Default: 1.35 Hz, matching DAFx-19 Section 2.1)\n   - Taper: Direct frequency in Hz (includes the physical regenerative phase-shift oscillator drift with Intensity).\n   - Function: Controls the speed of the incandescent lamp pulsation.\n\n2. lamp_intensity\n   - Range: 0.0 to 1.0 (Incandescent Lamp Brightness & LDR Sweep Asymmetry, Default: 0.78)\n   - Taper: Nonlinear CdS photoconductor response curve (DAFx-19 Fig. 8).\n   - Function: Controls both the frequency sweep range of the 4 LDRs and the fast-turn-on / slow-turn-off \"heartbeat\" asymmetry of the lamp-LDR chamber.\n\n3. shelf_throb_cdc\n   - Range: 0.0 to 1.0 (Blocking Capacitor C_DC High-Shelf & Collector/Emitter Imbalance, Default: 0.75)\n   - Taper: Linear circuit authenticity control (DAFx-19 Section 3.1, Eq. 6–9, Fig. 6).\n   - Function: At 0.0, forces ideal unity-gain allpass stages; at 0.75–1.0, engages the exact measured Table 1 alpha/beta imbalance and C_DC high-shelf response responsible for the Uni-Vibe's deep low-end throb.\n\n4. emitter_bias_grit\n   - Range: 0.0 to 1.0 (Darlington Emitter-Follower Asymmetric Saturation & DC-Blocker, Default: 0.35)\n   - Taper: Linear drive + bias offset u (DAFx-19 Section 3.2.1, Fig. 7).\n   - Function: Adds warm even/odd transistor harmonic grit inside each of the 4 phase stages without DC clicks.\n\n5. stereo_chamber_spread\n   - Range: 0.0 to 1.0 (Left/Right Lamp-Chamber LDR Angular Offset, Default: 0.45)\n   - Taper: Linear phase offset (0 to 126 degrees).\n   - Function: Simulates placing Left and Right LDR banks on opposite walls of the reflective lamp chamber for a swirling 3D stereo throb.\n\n6. chorus_to_vibrato\n   - Range: 0.0 to 1.0 (0.0 = Uni-Vibe Chorus Mode, 1.0 = Uni-Vibe Vibrato Mode; Default: 0.0)\n   - Taper: Linear crossfade modeling the resistive output mixer switch (DAFx-19 Section 2).\n   - Function: Switches or blends between deep phase-canceling rotary Chorus (0.0) and pure pitch-swept Vibrato (1.0).\n",
                    "varname": "paraminfo_comment"
                }
            }
        ],
